knext bundled fix for vinext 1.1.0 (dist output)
Upstream: https://github.com/cloudflare/vinext/pull/3768 (issues https://github.com/cloudflare/vinext/issues/2831 and https://github.com/cloudflare/vinext/issues/2810; builds on https://github.com/cloudflare/vinext/pull/2839)
What it fixes: Next.js 16.3 made its new App Router scroll handler the default, and vinext still
follows the legacy one, so three Next.js deploy-test files fail (navigation-focus, router-autoscroll,
parallel-routes-scroll-owner). After a client navigation:
  - focus is left alone (the legacy handler focused the navigated segment);
  - the route content's highest client rect is measured against the root `scroll-padding-top`,
    across the content's host siblings, so a fixed / sticky / display:none first child no longer
    picks the target and a React-hoisted first resource in <head> no longer suppresses the scroll
    to the top;
  - an intercepted route hands the scroll to its parallel slot: the retained page neither scrolls
    nor blurs, and the document-top fallback does not run on the slot's behalf. The navigation
    controller passes `parallelSlotOwned` (the committed navigation is an interception) to
    claimAppRouterScrollIntentForCommit, and the scroll target consumes the intent without scrolling.
Hash targets still scroll from any segment. Parallel slots are not scroll targets themselves, and
React 19 stable has no Fragment refs, so the route content is found with findDOMNode() plus its
following siblings. The head-hoisted suppression state (`headElements`, `targetHoistedInHead`,
markAppRouterScrollIntentHeadHoisted) existed only for the legacy handler and is removed.
Applies on top of every earlier patch in manifest order.
Retire: delete this file (and its manifest entry) when the pinned vinext is >= the first
release that includes cloudflare/vinext#3768.
Ported by hand from the upstream src change to the published 1.1.0 dist files.
Applied by `knext vinext-patches` (runs from the app's postinstall and before `knext build`).
